<%# The rest of a table row, for the width where the table is one column.

    Below `lg` every table in this app shows its first column and nothing else: the other cells are
    `hidden lg:table-cell`, and their values come back here as labelled lines inside the primary cell.
    That is the trading floor's treatment, generalised - it was the only table in the app that fitted
    375px, because it was the only one that collapsed instead of scrolling. Nine tables scrolled between
    196px and 685px sideways, which on a phone means the data is behind a gesture nobody is told about.

    A description list, because a label and a value is what a `<dl>` is for, and because `sr-only` labels
    would leave a column of bare values with nothing saying which is which. The `<th>`s stay with their
    columns and disappear with them: a header for a column nobody can see names nothing.

    Values may be pre-rendered markup - a badge, a link, an em dash for absent - so they arrive already
    escaped or already safe from the caller.

    Locals:
      fields  - array of [label, value] pairs, in the order the columns appear
      actions - optional pre-rendered row actions, placed after the fields %>
<% fields = local_assigns[:fields] || [] %>
<%# `lg:hidden` is the whole point in a table - this is the row's other columns, and above `lg` they are
    back in their own cells. It makes the component undemonstrable in the gallery, though, where a desktop
    reader would meet an empty box. `always:` drops the hiding for that one caller rather than the gallery
    keeping a hand-copied twin, which is the way these drift apart. %>
<% hide_above_lg = local_assigns.fetch(:always, false) ? nil : "lg:hidden" %>

<%# **A two-column list, not two edges.** This was `flex justify-between` with a right-aligned value, so
    the label sat against the left edge of the card and its value against the right. Measured at 375px:
    **174 to 244px of empty space** between the two, and because right-alignment ragged them, the values'
    left edges landed anywhere from 252px to 307px - a 55px spread. Eight fields to a transaction row is
    eight traversals of that gap against a column that never lines up. Reported as hard to parse, and it
    is.

    Three treatments, measured rather than argued:

    | | label to value | value left edges | row height |
    | opposed (was) | 174-244px | 252-307px, ragged | ~341px |
    | stacked | - | 33px, aligned | 477px |
    | **fixed label column** | **12px** | **157px, identical** | **333px** |

    The fixed column wins on every axis, which is why it is not a compromise between the other two.

    **When the field disagrees, it is about key length.** GOV.UK's summary list and Polaris's description
    list both *stack* below this width, and both are built for keys that are whole questions. Ours are one
    to three short words - the longest across all callers are "Price per share" and "Portfolio value" -
    and for short keys a two-column list is what Carbon's structured list and every native settings list
    do. `w-28` holds both of those at `text-xs`; a longer label wraps inside its column and the value
    column does not move, which is the graceful case.

    Label-left/value-right is iOS Settings, and it works there because those values are one short word.
    Ours are money, dates, tickers and badges. %>
<% if fields.any? %>
  <%= tag.dl class: ["mt-2 space-y-2", hide_above_lg] do %>
    <% fields.each do |label, value| %>
      <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
        <dt class="w-28 shrink-0 text-xs text-slate-600"><%= label %></dt>
        <dd class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-sm text-slate-900"><%= value %></dd>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<%# Row actions sit after the data, not before it: below lg they are the last thing in the row, which is
    where they are at lg too. `flex-wrap` because three labelled ghosts are ~250px and the cell is ~300px
    minus its padding, so the third can need a second line. %>
<% if local_assigns[:actions].present? %>
  <%# The testid is how a test tells a row *action* from any other link in the row. Since the record's
      name became a link in its own right, `tbody tr a` no longer means "a row action" - and the test
      that measures their height and icons was reading the 17px name link as one. %>
  <%= tag.div class: ["mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-1", hide_above_lg],
              data: { testid: "stacked-row-actions" } do %><%= actions %><% end %>
<% end %>
